# The leptosporangiate ferns (Polypodiidae): crowns of fronds on a
# rhizome, after the sword, lady, wood and deer ferns and bracken of wet
# forests. Each year a crown sends up fronds_per_year fronds round it on
# a golden-angle spiral. A frond is a rachis of `pieces` pieces, the
# first `stipe` share of its length bare, the rest carrying a pair of
# pinnae at each half piece, longest at pinna_peak along the blade and
# tapering to pinna_base and pinna_tip.
#
# A frond unrolls from a crozier: it emerges at a phase e of the year
# (uniform in [0, emerge_spread)), and at the plant's keyframe, season of
# the way through the year, it has unrolled the share
#
#   u = clamp((age + season - e) / unroll, 0, 1)
#
# of its length; the blade beyond u is wound into a crozier (revision 11)
# and its pinnae are folded small. As the frond ages it opens from young_angle
# to old_angle off the vertical, and it sags under its own weight: a
# tropism toward the ground of elasticity droop * min(a, droop_years) for
# its age a since it emerged, so old fronds arch over further. A frond
# dies after frond_life years (1 for a deciduous fern; about 2.5 for an
# evergreen one, whose fronds outlast a winter).
#
# A frond's length follows the crown's age, so a young fern's fronds are
# small:
#
#   l(a) = frond_length (frond_seed + (1 - frond_seed) (1 - exp(-a / size_years)))
#
# A fern spreads by its rhizome: from crown_age, with the chance crown_rate
# a year, a new crown appears rhizome metres away, at most `crowns` of them
# (bracken runs far; a tufted fern makes a clump a few crowns wide). A
# new crown starts crown_start of its parent crown's age: 0 a sporeling,
# whose first fronds are small, 1 a crown the established rhizome feeds,
# whose fronds are as large as its parent's.
#
# Revision 2 (spec rework M7): each frond is drawn within a `frond`
# structure, and its pinnae are leaflets of it.
#
# Revision 3 (growth plan G2.3a): the ferns' own program, renamed from
# `fern` and homed at Polypodiidae, whose fronds uncoil from croziers;
# its families and genera extend it where they grow otherwise. It grows
# every plant as before.
#
# Revision 4 (spec rework E6a): a new crown may start as old as its
# parent (crown_start); by default it starts as a sporeling, as before.
#
# Revision 5 (plants fitted to their place): a frond that sags to the
# ground lies on it, `rest_gap` above the substrate (substrate@1's, or
# level soil), instead of passing into it.
#
# Revision 6 (leaf life): fronds live as long as their parameters say, no
# longer one year more. What a step makes is born at the step's end, so a
# rule that ends a part by its age counts the step it is in (`age + dt
# >=`).
#
# Revision 7 (visual forms ferns): a frond arches as it unrolls, its
# rachis bending toward the ground by about `arch` degrees over its length
# (a pull that eases as it points down, so it never curls back), as fronds
# do (Hoshizaki and Moran 2001, p. 391: by default fronds bend outward and
# droop toward the tip), not only by the sag of its later years.
#
# A crown is no stalk: its growing point rises crown_rise a year unseen,
# under the bases round it, and each frond stands on its own foot up to
# crown_radius from the crown's centre, out toward the side it opens to,
# so the fronds fountain from a compact, uneven clump at the ground and no
# cylinder joins them to the soil. A frond comes up as a fiddlehead near
# the centre and its foot moves out as it opens. A coiled piece is short
# as well as curled back, so the fiddlehead is a tight coil that
# lengthens as it unrolls. Once a frond dies its stipe base stays stub_life
# years, a short stub leaning out among the living fronds' feet, so an
# old crown is clothed in old stipe bases (Dryopteris, p. 297).
#
# Revision 8 (visual forms fern crown): a rachis narrows from its foot to
# rachis_tip of its foot's radius at its tip, and a small frond's is
# thinner, as the square root of its length's share of a full frond. A
# stipe may be scaly: `scales` scales round each of its pieces, pressed
# toward its tip scale_angle degrees off it, scale_length long at its
# foot and scale_top of that at the stipe's top, as floras word a
# fern's scales, "gradually diminishing in size distally" (Flora of
# North America, Polystichum munitum). A stipe base that outlives its
# frond keeps its scales, so an old crown is a shaggy clump of them.
#
# Revision 9 (visual forms fern stipes): a stipe's scales may stand only
# on its foot, as floras word most ferns' ("scales usually persistent at
# base", Flora of North America, Dryopteridaceae): they go scale_reach of
# the way up it, diminishing to scale_top of their size there.
# A scaly piece is drawn as one segment, as a bare one is, so scales no
# longer make a stipe bend further (three segments took the tropism
# three times).
#
# Revision 10 (visual forms fern dimorphism): a fern may bear two kinds of
# frond, as floras word a dimorphic fern's (Flora of North America,
# Blechnum spicant: "Leaves dimorphic ... fertile leaves more erect and
# longer than sterile leaves"). fertile_fronds of each year's fronds are
# fertile: fertile_length long with fertile_stipe of it bare, standing at
# fertile_angle with fertile_arch and fertile_droop, from nearer the
# crown's centre, and bearing `fertile_pinna` pinnae (their own organ,
# so their own look: a fertile pinna is often narrow, contracted or
# brown with sporangia), the longest fertile_pinna_length of the frond.
# Each defaults to its sterile frond's, so a fern stated alike draws as
# before.

# Revision 11 (visual forms fiddleheads): a frond comes up as a crozier,
# its blade coiled from the tip downward toward its upper face and
# unrolling from its foot, so its lower pinnae open while its tip is
# still coiled (Hoshizaki and Moran 2001, p. 17; Kaplan 2022, p. 358-359,
# 369). The blade beyond the share unrolled winds coil_turns turns in all,
# each coiled piece shorter the nearer the tip (exp(-coil_tight d) of
# coil_size of its length, d its distance beyond the share unrolled), so
# the coil is a tight inward spiral, not a ring; each coiled piece is
# four short segments, so the spiral is round. A coiled pinna is small
# (coil_pinna of its size at the outer turn, less inward) and pressed
# toward the frond's tip and upper face, inside the coil, as each pinna
# coils as its frond does (Kaplan 2022, p. 352). A crozier is "often
# protected by hairs or scales" (Hoshizaki and Moran 2001, p. 17): a
# scaly fern's coil carries rings of its scales. The stipe does not
# coil: a piece of it not yet grown is short, so a new crozier stands
# low and rises on its lengthening stipe. Fronds that are not circinate
# (coil_turns 0) open straight. `curl`, a coiled piece's turn, is gone.

# Revision 12 (visual forms croziers): a frond is drawn young (`young`)
# until it has unrolled and turns to its stipe's colour over as long
# again, so a form whose appearance gives a young_axis colour draws its
# croziers and new stipes green and its summer stipes as before. A
# crozier carries rings of scales only where the stipe is scaly all
# along, so a fern scaly at its base alone has a smooth coil. A coiled
# pinna lies closer along the coil (25 degrees off the rachis and 60
# toward its upper face, from 45 and 30), so the pinnae hug the coil's
# sides instead of standing out from it as small open fronds. A ternate
# frond (basal_coils 1, bracken's) coils its two lowest pinnae on their
# own beside the blade's coil while its first piece of blade is coiled,
# so it comes up as three coils, an eagle's claw.
#
# Revision 13 (visual forms rolled pinnae): each pinna in the crozier is
# itself rolled, as its frond is (Kaplan 2022, p. 352): while coiled, a
# pinna is drawn as a short green coil of its own (Pk), as long as the
# blade's coiled pieces shrink it and coil_roll of that thick, curling
# toward the frond's upper face, so the crozier is a packed knot of small
# coils on both sides of its rachis instead of a bare coil with tiny open
# pinnae. Its card shrinks to a fifth as it rolls and grows back as it
# unrolls, so every pinna is still drawn and counted.
#
# Revision 14 (Phase C batch 3 gaps): a pinna is sized by where it is
# drawn on the blade, the lowest at pinna_base of the longest and the
# topmost at pinna_tip (before, by a place half a piece below it, so a
# short blade's lowest pair was never its longest), and pinna_peak 0 no
# longer divides by zero. A pedate blade (`pedate`) forks atop the stipe
# into two equal arms that spread level, each arm_length of a pinnate
# rachis long, curving back arm_turn degrees and bearing its pinnae on
# its outer side, so the blade is a flat fan (Abrams 1923, p. 24, the
# western maidenhair: "pinnae somewhat pedately arranged, arising from
# the outer side of 2 equal branches surmounting the stipe"). A scaly
# piece of stipe is drawn in three segments, a ring of scales at the foot
# of each, and bends under a third of the frond's elasticity at each, as
# much in all as a bare piece: before, the rings were set back along a
# piece in a straight line from its end, after the tropism had turned
# the heading, so on a long piece of an arching frond they stood off it.

# One step is one year. Angles are degrees; lengths are metres.

lsystem polypodiidae 14;

param vigour_spread = 0.12;   # a fern grows exp(gauss() * vigour_spread) times as fast
# Fronds.
param fronds_per_year = 8;
param frond_life = 1;         # years a frond lives
param frond_length = 0.6;     # a full-size frond, stipe included
param frond_seed = 0.2;       # a young fern's fronds, as a share of full size
param size_years = 3;         # fronds reach full size over about this many years
param frond_spread = 0.12;    # fronds are l (1 ± frond_spread) long
param young_angle = 15;       # angle of a new frond to the vertical
param old_angle = 55;         # the angle an old frond opens to
param open_years = 0.5;       # how fast a frond opens, years
param angle_spread = 8;       # spread of each frond's angle
param frond_radius = 0.002;   # the rachis' radius at its foot
param pieces = 12;            # pieces of the rachis
param stipe = 0.25;           # share of the frond's length without pinnae
param rachis_tip = 0.35;      # the rachis' radius at its tip, as a share of its foot's
# Scales on the stipe.
param scales = 0;             # scales round each piece of the stipe; none by default
param scale_length = 0.012;   # a scale at the stipe's foot, m
param scale_top = 0.4;        # a scale at the top of the scaly share, as a share of one at its foot
param scale_reach = 1;        # share of the stipe, from its foot, that bears scales
param scale_angle = 12;       # how far a scale stands off the stipe
param palea_area;             # leaf area of a scale 1 m long, m²; its look's by default
# Pinnae.
param pinna_length = 0.2;     # the longest pinna, as a share of the frond's length
param pinna_peak = 0.3;       # where it sits along the blade, 0 to 1
param pinna_base = 0.5;       # the lowest pinna, as a share of the longest
param pinna_tip = 0.1;        # the topmost pinna, as a share of the longest
param pinna_angle = 70;       # angle of a pinna to the rachis
param pinna_lift = 10;        # degrees the pinnae rise out of the frond's plane
param pinna_area = 0.23;      # leaf area of a pinna 1 m long, m²
param pedate = 0;             # 1: the blade forks atop the stipe into two arms bearing pinnae on their outer sides
param arm_spread = 90;        # how far each arm of a pedate blade turns off the frond's heading at the fork
param arm_turn = 90;          # how far each arm curves back over its length
param arm_length = 0.3;       # each arm of a pedate blade, as a share of a pinnate blade's rachis
# Fertile fronds, where they differ from the sterile ones; a negative
# value takes the sterile frond's.
param fertile_fronds = 0;     # share of each year's fronds that are fertile
param fertile_length = -1;    # a full-size fertile frond, stipe included
param fertile_stipe = -1;     # its share without pinnae
param fertile_angle = -1;     # the angle it opens to
param fertile_arch = -1;      # how far it bends over its length once unrolled
param fertile_droop = -1;     # its sag per year
param fertile_pinna_length = -1;  # its longest pinna, as a share of its length
param fertile_pinna_area;     # leaf area of a fertile pinna 1 m long; its look's by default
# Unrolling and sagging.
param season = 0.5;           # share of the year at the keyframe
param emerge_spread = 0.3;    # fronds emerge over this share of the year
param unroll = 0.25;          # share of a year a frond takes to unroll
param coil_turns = 2.5;       # turns of a frond's blade coiled in its crozier; 0 for one that expands straight
param coil_size = 0.22;       # a coiled piece's length at the crozier's outer turn, as a share of its open length
param coil_tight = 2;         # how fast the coiled pieces shorten toward the tip: exp(-coil_tight d)
param coil_pinna = 0.12;      # a coiled pinna at the crozier's outer turn, as a share of its open size
param coil_roll = 0.08;       # a rolled pinna's thickness, a share of its rolled length
param basal_coils = 0;        # 1: a ternate frond's lowest pinnae coil on their own beside its blade's coil
param droop = 0.02;           # sag per year of a frond's age
param arch = 60;              # about how far a frond's rachis bends toward the ground over its length once unrolled
param crown_radius = 0.02;    # fronds stand round a crown up to this far from its centre
param crown_rise = 0.002;     # how far a crown's growing point rises each year, unseen
param stub = 0.08;            # a stipe base, as a share of its frond's length
param stub_life = 3;          # years a stipe base outlives its frond
param droop_years = 2;        # sag stops growing after this many years
param rest_gap = 0.02;        # a frond that reaches the ground lies this far above it
# The rhizome.
param crowns = 0;             # most new crowns a fern sends out
param crown_age = 3;          # the first year of new crowns
param crown_rate = 0.3;       # chance a year of a new crown
param rhizome = 0.3;          # how far away a new crown appears
param crown_start = 0;        # a new crown's age, as a share of its parent crown's

module Y(v, n, c0);           # a crown: vigour, crowns sent out so far, age it started at
module Fc(i, m, c);           # fronds i to i + m - 1 of this year's cohort, on a crown c years old
module Fr(s, a, e, fe);        # a frond s long at a degrees, emerging at phase e of its year; fe 1 if fertile
module Pc(k, m, s, e, fe);     # pieces k to k + m - 1 of a frond's rachis
module Pp(k, s, e, fe);        # a drawn piece of a rachis
module Pr(k, s, fe, d, w, r, g); # a drawn piece of a rachis, d beyond the share unrolled, of a frond w of it stipe, r in radius, bending under elasticity g
module Ar(k, m, s, fe, q, w, ar); # pieces k to k + m - 1 of a pedate blade's arm, q the piece's place less d, ar 1 the left arm, -1 the right
module Pw(a, q, r, z);        # half a coiled piece: two segments q long turned a degrees, crozier scales z long
module Ck(z, r);              # a ring of crozier scales z long round a rachis r in radius
module Pn(k, s, fe, h, d, w, r, ar); # pinnae h of a piece along piece k, on a rachis r thick: a pair (ar 0), or one on an arm's outer side
module Pq(z, fe, c, r, ar);    # a pair of pinnae z long, c coiled, on a rachis r thick (ar 0), or one on the side away from ar
module Ph(z, fe, c, r, o);     # one pinna z long, c coiled, on a rachis r thick, turned to the side o (1 left, -1 right)
module Pb(k, s, fe, d, w, r);  # the first pair of pinnae on a coiled piece k
module Bw(q);                 # a coiled basal pinna, its first piece q long
module Pk(q, r);              # a rolled pinna, its first piece q long and r thick
module Cn(n, c);              # a new crown on its rhizome, the n-th, starting c years old
module Sb(s, a);              # a frond's stipe base, its frond s long, opening at a degrees
module Fe(l, e, fe);           # a frond l long and its foot, emerging at phase e; fe 1 if fertile
module Sk(z, r, n, o);        # n scales z long in a ring round a stipe r in radius, turned o degrees
module Sq(z, r, l, g);        # a piece of stipe l long and r in radius, scales z long along it, bending under elasticity g
module Fb(r, j, e);           # a frond's foot, r of crown_radius out at j degrees from its side, its frond emerging at phase e

organ pinna leaf as leaflet area pinna_area;
organ palea leaf as palea area palea_area;
organ fertile_pinna leaf as leaflet area fertile_pinna_area;

structure frond leaf;         # one frond: its rachis and its pinnae

axiom /(uniform(0, 360)) Y(exp(gauss() * vigour_spread), 0, 0);

# The crown: this year's fronds, and perhaps a new crown on the rhizome.
rule Y(v, n, c0) : crowns > n && age >= crown_age && rand() < crown_rate ->
    [ Cn(n + 1, (age + c0) * crown_start) ]
    f(crown_rise)
    Fc(0, max(1, round(fronds_per_year * v)), (age + c0) * v) Y(v, n + 1, c0);
rule Y(v, n, c0) ->
    f(crown_rise)
    Fc(0, max(1, round(fronds_per_year * v)), (age + c0) * v) Y(v, n, c0);

decompose Fc(i, m, c) : m > 1 ->
    Fc(i, floor(m / 2), c) Fc(i + floor(m / 2), m - floor(m / 2), c);
# A frond is fertile with the chance fertile_fronds; a fern with none
# draws no extra number, so it grows as before.
decompose Fc(i, m, c) : m == 1 && fertile_fronds > 0 ->
    /(137.5 + gauss() * 4)
    Fe(frond_length * (frond_seed + (1 - frond_seed) * (1 - exp(-c / size_years))),
       uniform(0, emerge_spread), rand() < fertile_fronds ? 1 : 0);
decompose Fc(i, m, c) : m == 1 ->
    /(137.5 + gauss() * 4)
    Fe(frond_length * (frond_seed + (1 - frond_seed) * (1 - exp(-c / size_years))),
       uniform(0, emerge_spread), 0);
decompose Fc(i, m, c) -> ;

# A frond on its foot, and the stipe base that stays when it dies.
# A fertile frond stands nearer the crown's centre.
decompose Fe(l, e, fe) ->
    [ Fb(uniform(0.3, 1) * (fe > 0.5 ? 0.5 : 1), gauss() * 25, e)
      [ Fr(l * uniform(1 - frond_spread, 1 + frond_spread)
             * (fe > 0.5 && fertile_length > 0 ? fertile_length / frond_length : 1),
           gauss() * angle_spread, e, fe) ]
      Sb(l, old_angle) ];
# A frond dies after frond_life years; its rachis pieces with it. Its stipe
# base stays stub_life years more.
rule Pc(k, m, s, e, fe) : age + dt >= frond_life -> ;
rule frond : age + dt >= frond_life -> ;
rule Sb(s, a) : age + dt >= frond_life + stub_life -> ;
rule Fb(r, j, e) : age + dt >= frond_life + stub_life -> ;

# A frond: it opens from the vertical with age and sags as it ages, then its
# rachis, piece by piece.
decompose Fr(s, a, e, fe) ->
    frond !(frond_radius * sqrt(min(1, s / frond_length))) Pc(0, max(1, round(pieces)), s, e, fe);
decompose Pc(k, m, s, e, fe) : m > 1 ->
    Pc(k, floor(m / 2), s, e, fe) Pc(k + floor(m / 2), m - floor(m / 2), s, e, fe);

# One piece: the first opens and sags the frond, and marks it young (green,
# where the form gives a young_axis colour) until it has unrolled, fading
# over as long again; a piece beyond the unrolled share coils back, and its
# pinnae are folded small.
interpret Pc(k, m, s, e, fe) : k == 0 && fe > 0.5 ->
    &(lerp(min(young_angle, fertile_angle < 0 ? old_angle : fertile_angle),
           fertile_angle < 0 ? old_angle : fertile_angle,
           1 - exp(-max(0, age + season - e) / open_years)))
    T(0, -1, 0, (fertile_arch < 0 ? arch : fertile_arch) * pi / 180 / (1.5 * pieces) * clamp((age + season - e) / unroll, 0, 1)
               + (fertile_droop < 0 ? droop : fertile_droop) * min(max(0, age + season - e), droop_years)) rest(rest_gap)
    young(1 - smoothstep(1, 2, (age + season - e) / unroll))
    Pp(k, s, e, fe);
interpret Pc(k, m, s, e, fe) : k == 0 ->
    &(lerp(young_angle, old_angle, 1 - exp(-max(0, age + season - e) / open_years)))
    T(0, -1, 0, arch * pi / 180 / (1.5 * pieces) * clamp((age + season - e) / unroll, 0, 1)
               + droop * min(max(0, age + season - e), droop_years)) rest(rest_gap)
    young(1 - smoothstep(1, 2, (age + season - e) / unroll))
    Pp(k, s, e, fe);
interpret Pc(k, m, s, e, fe) -> Pp(k, s, e, fe);

# A piece of rachis: Pr knows d, how far the piece lies beyond the share
# unrolled (as a share of the frond), w, the frond's stipe share, and r,
# the rachis' radius there.
interpret Pp(k, s, e, fe) ->
    Pr(k, s, fe, (k + 0.5) / pieces - clamp((age + season - e) / unroll, 0, 1),
       fe > 0.5 && fertile_stipe >= 0 ? fertile_stipe : stipe,
       frond_radius * sqrt(min(1, s / frond_length)) * lerp(1, rachis_tip, (k + 0.5) / pieces),
       (fe > 0.5 && fertile_arch >= 0 ? fertile_arch : arch) * pi / 180 / (1.5 * pieces) * clamp((age + season - e) / unroll, 0, 1)
       + (fe > 0.5 && fertile_droop >= 0 ? fertile_droop : droop) * min(max(0, age + season - e), droop_years));

# The stipe does not coil: a piece of it not yet grown is short, so a new
# crozier stands low and rises as its stipe lengthens.
interpret Pr(k, s, fe, d, w, r, g) : (k + 0.5) / pieces < w * scale_reach ->
    !(r) Sq(scale_length * lerp(1, scale_top, (k + 0.5) / pieces / (w * scale_reach)), r,
            s / pieces * lerp(1, 0.15, smoothstep(0, 0.15, d)), g);
interpret Pr(k, s, fe, d, w, r, g) : (k + 0.5) / pieces < w ->
    !(r) F(s / pieces * lerp(1, 0.15, smoothstep(0, 0.15, d)));
# A coiled piece of blade: four short segments, each turned toward the
# frond's upper face, a quarter of the piece's share of coil_turns, and
# shorter the nearer the tip, so the crozier winds inward as a tight
# spiral. Its scales clothe it, and no tropism bends it.
# A pedate blade: at its first piece the frond turns level and forks
# into its two arms, which draw the blade's pieces; the pieces after it
# along the frond draw nothing.
interpret Pr(k, s, fe, d, w, r, g) : pedate >= 1 && k <= ceil(w * pieces - 0.5) ->
    face(0, 1, 0, 1) T(0, -1, 0, 0)
    [ +(arm_spread) Ar(k, max(1, pieces - k), s, fe, d - (k + 0.5) / pieces, w, 1) ]
    [ -(arm_spread) Ar(k, max(1, pieces - k), s, fe, d - (k + 0.5) / pieces, w, -1) ];
interpret Pr(k, s, fe, d, w, r, g) : pedate >= 1 -> ;
interpret Pr(k, s, fe, d, w, r, g) : d > 0.075 && coil_turns > 0 ->
    T(0, -1, 0, 0) !(r)
    Pw(coil_turns * 360 / max(1, pieces * (1 - w)) * smoothstep(0, 0.15, d) / 4,
       s / pieces * lerp(1, coil_size * exp(-coil_tight * d), smoothstep(0, 0.15, d)) / 4,
       r, scale_length * scale_top * 0.7 * sqrt(min(1, s / frond_length)))
    Pb(k, s, fe, d, w, r)
    Pw(coil_turns * 360 / max(1, pieces * (1 - w)) * smoothstep(0, 0.15, d) / 4,
       s / pieces * lerp(1, coil_size * exp(-coil_tight * d), smoothstep(0, 0.15, d)) / 4,
       r, scale_length * scale_top * 0.7 * sqrt(min(1, s / frond_length)))
    Pn(k, s, fe, 0.5, d, w, r, 0);
# A piece of blade unrolled, or still turning as it opens.
interpret Pr(k, s, fe, d, w, r, g) ->
    !(r)
    ^(coil_turns * 360 / max(1, pieces * (1 - w)) * smoothstep(0, 0.15, d))
    F(s / pieces * 0.5 * lerp(1, coil_size * exp(-coil_tight * max(0, d)), smoothstep(0, 0.15, d)))
    Pn(k, s, fe, 0, d, w, r, 0)
    F(s / pieces * 0.5 * lerp(1, coil_size * exp(-coil_tight * max(0, d)), smoothstep(0, 0.15, d)))
    Pn(k, s, fe, 0.5, d, w, r, 0);

# An arm of a pedate blade, piece by piece: each arm_length of a piece
# of a pinnate blade's rachis, so the arms are short beside the long
# pinnae they bear, turning back arm_turn over the arm, a pinna on its
# outer side at each half piece; a piece beyond the share unrolled
# curls and shrinks as a pinnate blade's does.
interpret Ar(k, m, s, fe, q, w, ar) : m > 1 ->
    Ar(k, floor(m / 2), s, fe, q, w, ar) Ar(k + floor(m / 2), m - floor(m / 2), s, fe, q, w, ar);
interpret Ar(k, m, s, fe, q, w, ar) ->
    +(ar * arm_turn / max(1, pieces - ceil(w * pieces - 0.5)))
    !(frond_radius * sqrt(min(1, s / frond_length)) * lerp(1, rachis_tip, (k + 0.5) / pieces))
    ^(coil_turns * 360 / max(1, pieces * (1 - w)) * smoothstep(0, 0.15, (k + 0.5) / pieces + q))
    F(s / pieces * 0.5 * arm_length * lerp(1, coil_size * exp(-coil_tight * max(0, (k + 0.5) / pieces + q)), smoothstep(0, 0.15, (k + 0.5) / pieces + q)))
    Pn(k, s, fe, 0, (k + 0.5) / pieces + q, w, frond_radius * sqrt(min(1, s / frond_length)) * lerp(1, rachis_tip, (k + 0.5) / pieces), ar)
    F(s / pieces * 0.5 * arm_length * lerp(1, coil_size * exp(-coil_tight * max(0, (k + 0.5) / pieces + q)), smoothstep(0, 0.15, (k + 0.5) / pieces + q)))
    Pn(k, s, fe, 0.5, (k + 0.5) / pieces + q, w, frond_radius * sqrt(min(1, s / frond_length)) * lerp(1, rachis_tip, (k + 0.5) / pieces), ar);

# Half a coiled piece: two segments q long, each turned a degrees, with a
# ring of crozier scales z long at the end of each where the stipe is
# scaly all along, so its scales go on up the rachis.
interpret Pw(a, q, r, z) -> ^(a) F(q) Ck(z, r) ^(a) F(q) Ck(z, r);
interpret Ck(z, r) : scales >= 1 && scale_reach >= 1 -> [ Sk(z, r, ceil(scales / 6), 0) ];
interpret Ck(z, r) -> ;

# A frond's foot: out from the crown's centre toward the side the frond
# opens to (turned j degrees off it), from near the centre while it is a
# fiddlehead to r of crown_radius once open.
interpret Fb(r, j, e) ->
    /(j) &(90)
    f(crown_radius * r * lerp(0.3, 1, 1 - exp(-max(0, age + season - e) / open_years)))
    ^(90) \(j);

# A stipe base: once its frond has died, a short stub as thick as the
# rachis was at its foot, leaning out further than the frond stood and
# narrowing to its broken end.
interpret Sb(s, a) : age + dt < frond_life -> ;
interpret Sb(s, a) ->
    &(a + 10) !(frond_radius * 1.3) Sq(scale_length, frond_radius * 1.3, s * stub * 0.5, 0)
    &(8) !(frond_radius * 0.8) Sq(scale_length * 0.8, frond_radius * 0.8, s * stub * 0.5, 0);

# A piece of stipe clothed in its scales: three rings of a third of them
# along it, each turned from the last, so they overlap like shingles. The
# piece is drawn in three segments, a ring at the foot of each, so every
# scale stands where the stipe is; each segment bends under a third of
# the frond's elasticity g, so a scaly stipe bends as much as a bare one
# drawn in one (a tropism acts once a segment), and the frond's own
# elasticity is put back after it.
interpret Sq(z, r, l, g) : scales >= 1 ->
    T(0, -1, 0, g / 3)
    [ Sk(z, r, ceil(scales / 3), 0) ] F(l / 3)
    [ Sk(z, r, ceil(scales / 3), 40) ] F(l / 3)
    [ Sk(z, r, ceil(scales / 3), 80) ] F(l / 3)
    T(0, -1, 0, g);
interpret Sq(z, r, l, g) -> F(l);

# A ring of scales round a stipe, each from its surface and pressed toward
# its tip, turned a little at random; halved till one is left, so the
# ring nests no deeper than a few levels.
interpret Sk(z, r, n, o) : n >= 2 -> Sk(z, r, floor(n / 2), o) Sk(z, r, n - floor(n / 2), o);
interpret Sk(z, r, n, o) : n >= 1 ->
    [ /(o + uniform(-20, 20)) &(90) f(r) ^(90 - scale_angle) palea(z * uniform(0.7, 1.3)) ]
    /(360 / ceil(scales / 3));
interpret Sk(z, r, n, o) -> ;

# Pinnae half a piece along: sized by where they stand along the blade,
# b from 0 at its lowest pinnae (on the first piece past the stipe,
# ceil(w pieces - 0.5)) to 1 at its topmost. While coiled a pinna is small and
# folded against the rachis toward the frond's upper face, inside the
# crozier, as each pinna coils as its frond does (Kaplan 2022, p. 352).
interpret Pn(k, s, fe, h, d, w, r, ar) : ar == 0 ->
    Pq(s * (fe > 0.5 && fertile_pinna_length >= 0 ? fertile_pinna_length : pinna_length)
       * (clamp((k + h - ceil(w * pieces - 0.5)) / max(0.5, pieces - ceil(w * pieces - 0.5) - 0.5), 0, 1) < pinna_peak
          ? lerp(pinna_base, 1, clamp((k + h - ceil(w * pieces - 0.5)) / max(0.5, pieces - ceil(w * pieces - 0.5) - 0.5), 0, 1) / max(pinna_peak, 0.000001))
          : lerp(1, pinna_tip, (clamp((k + h - ceil(w * pieces - 0.5)) / max(0.5, pieces - ceil(w * pieces - 0.5) - 0.5), 0, 1) - pinna_peak) / max(1 - pinna_peak, 0.000001)))
       * lerp(1, coil_pinna * exp(-coil_tight * max(0, d)), smoothstep(0, 0.15, d + (h - 0.5) / pieces)),
       fe, smoothstep(0, 0.15, d + (h - 0.5) / pieces), r, 0);
# On a pedate blade's arm, one pinna, on the outer side, drawn as one
# side of a pair is.
interpret Pn(k, s, fe, h, d, w, r, ar) ->
    Ph(s * (fe > 0.5 && fertile_pinna_length >= 0 ? fertile_pinna_length : pinna_length)
       * (clamp((k + h - ceil(w * pieces - 0.5)) / max(0.5, pieces - ceil(w * pieces - 0.5) - 0.5), 0, 1) < pinna_peak
          ? lerp(pinna_base, 1, clamp((k + h - ceil(w * pieces - 0.5)) / max(0.5, pieces - ceil(w * pieces - 0.5) - 0.5), 0, 1) / max(pinna_peak, 0.000001))
          : lerp(1, pinna_tip, (clamp((k + h - ceil(w * pieces - 0.5)) / max(0.5, pieces - ceil(w * pieces - 0.5) - 0.5), 0, 1) - pinna_peak) / max(1 - pinna_peak, 0.000001)))
       * lerp(1, coil_pinna * exp(-coil_tight * max(0, d)), smoothstep(0, 0.15, d + (h - 0.5) / pieces)),
       fe, smoothstep(0, 0.15, d + (h - 0.5) / pieces), r, -ar);
# A ternate frond's lowest pinnae, nearly as large as the rest of its
# blade, coil on their own while the blade's first piece is coiled: three
# coils on the stipe, an eagle's claw. Each winds about two turns inward,
# toward the frond's upper face, in sixteen pieces each nine tenths of the
# last, its length about half coil_size of the pinna's.
interpret Pb(k, s, fe, d, w, r) : basal_coils > 0 && (k - 0.5) / pieces < w ->
    [ +(55) !(r * 0.5) Bw(s * (fe > 0.5 && fertile_pinna_length >= 0 ? fertile_pinna_length : pinna_length)
                          * pinna_base * coil_size / 16) ]
    [ -(55) !(r * 0.5) Bw(s * (fe > 0.5 && fertile_pinna_length >= 0 ? fertile_pinna_length : pinna_length)
                          * pinna_base * coil_size / 16) ];
interpret Pb(k, s, fe, d, w, r) -> Pn(k, s, fe, 0, d, w, r, 0);
interpret Bw(q) ->
    F(q) ^(45) F(q * 0.90) ^(45) F(q * 0.81) ^(45) F(q * 0.73)
    ^(45) F(q * 0.66) ^(45) F(q * 0.59) ^(45) F(q * 0.53) ^(45) F(q * 0.48)
    ^(45) F(q * 0.43) ^(45) F(q * 0.39) ^(45) F(q * 0.35) ^(45) F(q * 0.31)
    ^(45) F(q * 0.28) ^(45) F(q * 0.25) ^(45) F(q * 0.23) ^(45) F(q * 0.21);

# A pair of pinnae while coiled (c above 0): each card small, and beside
# it the pinna rolled, a coil as long as the blade's coiled pieces shrink
# it, both fading as the pinna unrolls.
interpret Pq(z, fe, c, r, ar) : c > 0.01 && coil_turns > 0 && fe > 0.5 ->
    [ +(lerp(pinna_angle, 25, c)) ^(lerp(pinna_lift, 60, c)) fertile_pinna(z * (1 - c * 0.8)) ]
    [ -(lerp(pinna_angle, 25, c)) ^(lerp(pinna_lift, 60, c)) fertile_pinna(z * (1 - c * 0.8)) ]
    [ +(lerp(pinna_angle, 25, c)) ^(lerp(pinna_lift, 30, c)) Pk(z * c * coil_size / coil_pinna / 4.2, min(r, z * c * coil_size / coil_pinna * coil_roll)) ]
    [ -(lerp(pinna_angle, 25, c)) ^(lerp(pinna_lift, 30, c)) Pk(z * c * coil_size / coil_pinna / 4.2, min(r, z * c * coil_size / coil_pinna * coil_roll)) ];
interpret Pq(z, fe, c, r, ar) : c > 0.01 && coil_turns > 0 ->
    [ +(lerp(pinna_angle, 25, c)) ^(lerp(pinna_lift, 60, c)) pinna(z * (1 - c * 0.8)) ]
    [ -(lerp(pinna_angle, 25, c)) ^(lerp(pinna_lift, 60, c)) pinna(z * (1 - c * 0.8)) ]
    [ +(lerp(pinna_angle, 25, c)) ^(lerp(pinna_lift, 30, c)) Pk(z * c * coil_size / coil_pinna / 4.2, min(r, z * c * coil_size / coil_pinna * coil_roll)) ]
    [ -(lerp(pinna_angle, 25, c)) ^(lerp(pinna_lift, 30, c)) Pk(z * c * coil_size / coil_pinna / 4.2, min(r, z * c * coil_size / coil_pinna * coil_roll)) ];
# A rolled pinna: six pieces, each turned 60 degrees toward the frond's
# upper face and 0.85 of the last, a coil of about one turn inward,
# thinning toward its tip.
interpret Pk(q, r) ->
    !(r) F(q) ^(60) F(q * 0.85) ^(60) F(q * 0.72) ^(60) !(r * 0.8) F(q * 0.61)
    ^(60) F(q * 0.52) ^(60) !(r * 0.6) F(q * 0.44);
interpret Pq(z, fe, c, r, ar) : fe > 0.5 ->
    [ +(lerp(pinna_angle, 25, c)) ^(lerp(pinna_lift, 60, c)) fertile_pinna(z) ]
    [ -(lerp(pinna_angle, 25, c)) ^(lerp(pinna_lift, 60, c)) fertile_pinna(z) ];
interpret Pq(z, fe, c, r, ar) ->
    [ +(lerp(pinna_angle, 25, c)) ^(lerp(pinna_lift, 60, c)) pinna(z) ]
    [ -(lerp(pinna_angle, 25, c)) ^(lerp(pinna_lift, 60, c)) pinna(z) ];
interpret Ph(z, fe, c, r, o) : c > 0.01 && coil_turns > 0 && fe > 0.5 ->
    [ +(o * lerp(pinna_angle, 25, c)) ^(lerp(pinna_lift, 60, c)) fertile_pinna(z * (1 - c * 0.8)) ]
    [ +(o * lerp(pinna_angle, 25, c)) ^(lerp(pinna_lift, 30, c)) Pk(z * c * coil_size / coil_pinna / 4.2, min(r, z * c * coil_size / coil_pinna * coil_roll)) ];
interpret Ph(z, fe, c, r, o) : c > 0.01 && coil_turns > 0 ->
    [ +(o * lerp(pinna_angle, 25, c)) ^(lerp(pinna_lift, 60, c)) pinna(z * (1 - c * 0.8)) ]
    [ +(o * lerp(pinna_angle, 25, c)) ^(lerp(pinna_lift, 30, c)) Pk(z * c * coil_size / coil_pinna / 4.2, min(r, z * c * coil_size / coil_pinna * coil_roll)) ];
interpret Ph(z, fe, c, r, o) : fe > 0.5 ->
    [ +(o * lerp(pinna_angle, 25, c)) ^(lerp(pinna_lift, 60, c)) fertile_pinna(z) ];
interpret Ph(z, fe, c, r, o) ->
    [ +(o * lerp(pinna_angle, 25, c)) ^(lerp(pinna_lift, 60, c)) pinna(z) ];

# A new crown: out along the rhizome, then up.
decompose Cn(n, c) ->
    /(uniform(0, 360)) &(90) f(rhizome * uniform(0.6, 1.4)) ^(90)
    Y(exp(gauss() * vigour_spread), n, c);
